<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as trionfi, tarocchi, tarocks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarot, first known as trionfi and later as tarocchi or tarocks, is a set of playing cards that first appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where the Fool and 21 trumps were added to the standard Italian pack of four suits. From at least the mid-15th century it was used to play trick-taking card games, which spread to most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and are still played, among them French Tarot and Austrian Königrufen. Its use for divination came later: the earliest evidence is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> from around 1750, and French occultists of the late 18th century made unsubstantiated claims about the cards' history and meaning, from which decks made for cartomancy arose.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Early European playing cards were probably based on the Egyptian Mamluk deck, and the first records of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> date to 1367 in Bern. The first documented tarot decks were recorded between 1440 and 1450 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, when additional trump cards with allegorical illustrations were added to the four-suit pack. The earliest documentation of the trionfi is a statement in the Florence court records of 1440 about the transfer of two decks to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismondo-pandolfo-malatesta">Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta</a>. The oldest surviving cards are the Visconti-Sforza Tarot, painted in the mid-15th century for the rulers of the Duchy of Milan. The name tarocchi first appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> around 1502.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>A tarot pack has four suits of 14 cards each, with a separate 21-card trump suit and the Fool. Italian decks use swords, batons, coins and cups, and French-suited ones clubs, diamonds, hearts and spades. Trump order varied by region, perhaps from the 1440s. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> a larger deck of 97 cards, Minchiate, added astrological symbols and the four elements. Tarot spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>, and the oldest Marseille deck is Philippe Vachier's of 1639.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Tarot was first a card game, and the earliest complete description of the rules of a French variant dates to 1637. The earliest evidence of divination is an anonymous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> from around 1750. In the 1780s Antoine Court and Jean-Baptiste Alliette, called Etteilla, popularised esoteric tarot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. French occultists claimed links to ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Kabbalah, claims for which scholars find no historical evidence. Michael Dummett called them the subject of the most successful propaganda campaign ever launched.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tarot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
